                <pb facs="0964" n="830" sameAs="frus:frus1945Berlinv01/pg830"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860h.00/7–1045: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 555<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-07-10T19:00:00+02:00">July 10, 1945—7
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>4134. Following is substance of remarks made to Lane today by Maček, president of
                    Croatian Peasant Party: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Yugo today is Communist dictatorship. No democracy in Anglo-Saxon sense
                            of word. Of eleven members comprising Croatian Federal Government at
                            Zagreb only two are of Croatian Peasant Party, remaining nine all
                            Communists. As Peasant Parties are strongest bulwark against Communism
                            they are being liquidated in Yugo, Bulgaria, Rumania, and Poland.
                            Compares future position of Mikołajczyk in Poland to present position of
                            Šubašić who has no power. Little by little prestige of Mik[ołajczyk]
                            will be diminished by Communists. Maćek has not been allowed to see
                            Šubašić nor is he aware of details of agreement between Tito and
                            Šubašić. Believes that if Yugo which never Communist inclined has been
                            dominated it will be far easier for Communists to dominate Italy and
                            France both of which are ripe for Communism. Considers European
                            situation desperate but still willing cooperate with Tito provided a
                            real democracy based on free elections were restored.</p>
                        <p>Anxious to proceed to London to consult King Peter and Krnjević but
                            British obstructing his trip.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
